      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "11"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2011",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues our study of divisions by 2. Therefore, before going further, we recommend reading the text *Two Sides* (<htcite0>) and reviewing its prerequisites.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nOnce again, let’s start by looking at ourselves. Figure 1 shows a person’s two hands, representing the two sides of the body:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgLH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgLH', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgRH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgRH', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><b>Figure 1</b>: Representation of the hands. © History Tracers. <span id=\"htImgCopyright\"></span></td></tr></table></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The figure shows a person's two hands raised with all fingers extended, each pointing toward a fruit.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now, place your hands on a table or hold them in front of you, and answer Reflection 1:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Illusions...</b><br /><br />When we think about the sides of the body, is there something beyond those two sides?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_dimensions",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Stepping Out of the Plane\n\nFigure 1 has already been used twice in *History Tracers*, when our goal was to show that the human body has two equal sides (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). However, the fact that those texts focused on this theme does not mean that this was all we could say about it.\n\n#### Going Deeper\n\nNow place your hands in the same position as in Graphic 1:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><i class=\"fa-solid fa-hands-holding\" style=\"font-size:20em;color:#000;\"></i></center></p><p class=\"desc\">Graphic 1: Hands positioned as if to grab something. Images available in the free version of Font Awesome (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ad0cdae3-2260-4462-86a7-0987c6ffc82b",
                     "text": "Font Awesome",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "06",
                        "day": "04"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Although this graphic still shows two hands, from this new viewing angle we can see that the two sides — which we can think of as dimensions — also reveal a third dimension, which we call depth. This “new” dimension has always been present, but, for purely didactic reasons, we ignored it. This leads us to a reflection:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Depth</b><br /><br />Imagine you had only two dimensions, with no depth at all.<br /><br />Would it be possible to do everything you do in your life?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Plane\n\nWhen we say that something has “only” two dimensions, as in the case of flat surfaces (<htcite0>), we are usually simplifying reality. The simplification is easy to spot: if we look around us, we cannot find anything thin enough to be reduced to two dimensions only. Even the sheets of paper we draw on have depth — small, but real.\n\n#### Mass\n\nSince we cannot remain restricted to two dimensions, we are led to deepen our knowledge of the expansion of the universe. Let us return to Figure 2, which shows this expansion according to our current understanding (<htcite1>; <htcite2>; <htcite3>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0cb685b3-30b9-4c4b-8b8a-cb777c51208a",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Plane",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6dfe4b3b-8338-46ab-8149-2e073414907e",
                     "text": "NASA",
                     "page": "Cosmic History",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "ESA",
                     "page": "History of Structure Formation",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "015c0ffc-d894-404a-b795-eebc8aec2744",
                     "text": "Lincoln, Don",
                     "page": "Cosmic Inflation",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img  id=\"imgESA2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgESA2', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 2</b>: Planck, The History of the Universe. ©ESA – C. Carreau (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "ESA",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "09",
                        "day": "17"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When the first particles and light appeared as energy transformed into matter, the particles acquired what we call mass. The additional dimension we call depth, however, had always been present. Light, on the other hand, has no mass.\n\nThe explanation for why particles have mass was proposed by Higgs in <htdate0> and confirmed at CERN laboratories in <htdate1>. According to Higgs, particles acquire mass by interacting with something invisible to our eyes that nevertheless affects them: a field. Light does not interact with this particular field, so it has no mass (<htcite0>). The fields present in the universe can be compared to our atmosphere: although we cannot see it, it is all around us, and we can measure some of its properties, such as wind speed, which helps us predict the weather (<htcite1>; <htcite2>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae8d4e6a-8916-4d56-97a5-af126bc7e620",
                     "text": "CERN",
                     "page": "The Higgs Boson",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "15779436-6cf9-40fa-8670-eb8927e2d432",
                     "text": "Strassler, Matt",
                     "page": "What are fields?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "3",
                        "day": "21"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "985c8aa3-2680-49bb-be25-505d786b1030",
                     "text": "Strassler, Matt",
                     "page": "Ch. 13, 14",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "3",
                        "day": "5"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1964",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2012",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: The Universe and Us</b><br /><br />We contain the same energy that was present in the universe at its beginning. We have mass and, therefore, more than one dimension, thanks to the way the universe evolved. In this way, we are part of the universe, just like everything around us.<br /><br />If everything we see has at least three dimensions, why should we limit our explanations to only two?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_3view",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Discovering the Dimensions\n\nIt is not difficult to find examples in which dividing things into only two dimensions fails to explain the whole. Still, some situations are so unsettling that we wish we could return to the simplicity of just two. Let us look at some examples.\n\n#### A Vision of Three Regions of the Universe\n\nMillennia ago, we stopped believing that only heaven and earth existed.\n\nMesoamerican peoples conceived of the universe as divided into heaven, earth, and the underworld. This worldview is represented in the Mesoamerican ballgame (Figure 3), as Aguilar-Moreno describes:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "87a40e3a-c09b-466a-8a15-37091ddd38a5",
                     "text": "Aguilar-Moreno",
                     "page": "pp. 76 - 78",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<ul class=\"citation\"><li><i>\"...Ball courts symbolized the universe, and the ball game itself was a metaphor for celestial movements—particularly those of the Sun, Moon, and Venus. The ball’s motion represented the Sun’s journey into and out of the underworld....\"</i></li> </ul>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"Copan\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('Copan', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 3</b>: View of the <i>Juego de Pelota de Copán</i>, Honduras. Photograph taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "30"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Worlds\n\nThe first European accounts of the so-called discovery of the American continent (*Abya Yala*) were extremely limited, as Amerigo Vespucci made clear in a letter to Lorenzo Pietro di Medici. Already in the opening paragraph of the letter we can see that, instead of describing a continent, he refers to it as a \"new world\" (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1c70359-55eb-4f7e-b332-11aa3de7035a",
                     "text": "Americo Vespucio",
                     "page": "pp. 1",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1916",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgMundus\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMundus', '0%')\" onkeydown=\"if(event.key==='Enter'||event.key===' '){event.preventDefault();htImageZoom('imgMundus', '0%');}\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Zoom image: Book 1 - Page 1 of the letter of Amerigo Vespucci\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Book 1</b>: Page 1 of the letter of Amerigo Vespucci (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1c70359-55eb-4f7e-b332-11aa3de7035a",
                     "text": "Americo Vespucio",
                     "page": "pp. 1",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1916",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Over time, this division proved insufficient. Depending on what we study — and on the territorial and social disputes of each period — new classifications became necessary. Let us look at Figure 4, for example.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgMWPWEEC\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMWPWEEC', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenterlarge\"/><b>Figure 4</b>: Cradles of civilization. © Mapswire (<htcite0>). Shared under the <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0) license.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "-1",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "This figure shows not only the continents, but also today’s countries. On top of them, and for didactic purposes, we highlighted the regions that gave rise to six cradles of civilization (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). Depending on what we study, other divisions could also be proposed.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "893e651f-8354-4d9a-b182-893512a3bbbe",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Cradles of civilization (Part I)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b8e8452c-55fc-4f5b-aa3c-10669fa3cd54",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Cradles of civilization (Part II)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 4: Separating the maps</b><br /><br />What other division would you make using the map in Figure 4?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Ideas\n\nWhen we talk about human ideas, we face the most complex case: accepting that two sides or two perspectives are never enough to explain something. Let us begin with a reflection:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 5: When two are, in fact, one</b><br /><br />Do you like being right?<br /><br />Do you like mastering something?<br /><br />Have you ever thought that sometimes we want something to have only two sides because it is easier to center everything on ourselves?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To better understand this reflection, let us return to the example of the sheet of paper divided in half, presented in the previous text (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Sheet divided</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Sheet divided into two parts.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Suppose these two sides represent two different ideas: one that you like and one that you do not. Now imagine that you start dividing each side into equal parts again, as we did before. Obviously, the more fragments appear, the harder it becomes to bring everything back into a single whole.\n\nHaving only two sides creates yet another problem: it imprisons ideas and people within desires that are not their own. After all, there have never been — and there never will be — two people exactly alike, as we will continue to study throughout this and the following chapters.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text we learned that we are part of a universe that has at least three dimensions, and that the model explaining why matter has mass was confirmed only in <htdate0> (<htcite0>). This implies that the universe — and we, who are made of the same matter that constitutes it — have at least three dimensions.\n\nWe also saw that we cannot reduce the universe to a simple plane, because of the depth it has. Likewise, although we have sometimes divided certain things into only two regions such as “heaven and Earth”, the advance of our knowledge and of our human interactions has repeatedly compelled us to create new divisions. This has led us, among other consequences, to having more than 190 countries in the world today.\n\nFinally, we understood that reducing something to only two sides can be a way of limiting ourselves and of going against our very own nature.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae8d4e6a-8916-4d56-97a5-af126bc7e620",
                     "text": "CERN",
                     "page": "The Higgs Boson",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2012",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Is everything in the universe flat?",
